\ul\f1 1.00 Introduction\ulnone\f0\par
\par
\f2 This is a technical discussion of some of the many flaws within OS/8, a fair quantity of which are not immediately apparent to end-users.\par
\par
\f1 Wherever possible, relevant details will be provided to allow the reader to gain insight into the various issue.  where relevant analogies will be made to the design of P?S/8 (which is, in part, an attempt to produce a better outcome on PDP-8 systems of a range of capabilities ranging from systems OS/8 cannot run on to larger systems where the availability of extended memory allows for a more powerful system when proper design techniques are applied.  In rare cases, compromises in the P?S/8 design will also be discussed to provide additional insight.\par
\par
On many levels, OS/8 represents the weaknesses of a mediocre design which was prematurely fixated due to management interference; as such, many of these problems can never be rectified.  Since P?S/8 is an independent effort (actually started \b prior\b0  to OS/8, and ironically by some of the same programmers) design changes have always been embraced.  If something was implemented and it was revealed it needed to be scrapped in favor of something better, the changes were made, time permitting.\par
\par
Since this document is being written both intermittently and without an overall outline, the author reserves the right to totally reorganize this work in the future (in many ways mimicking the P?S/8 design methods).\par
\par
\ul 1.10 Earlier single-user PDP-8 operating systems predating OS/8\par
\par
\ulnone There were technically two such offerings by DEC:\line\par
1) The DECtape Library System.\line\line 2) The Disk Monitor System.\par
\par
Both of these systems, despite being specifically designed for a 4K system, contain features that unduly influenced OS/8:\line\line a) The DECtape Library System only runs on a 4K PDP-8 system with\par
   TC01/TC08 DECtape and a TU55/TU56 DECtape drive.\par
\par
   Note: Only drive unit 0 is supported.\par
\par
   The only purpose served by this earliest DEC effort is to be booted\par
   while any portion of user memory (00000-07577) contains a program\par
   deemed worthy of saving for faster retrieval later.\page\par
   In general, this pertains to memory loaded using the paper-tape\par
   operating system; the most basic improvement being to save images\par
   of the paper-tape operating system components such as PAL III,\par
   EDIT, etc.  The computer is still being used solely for paper-tape\par
   development; however, changing which paper-tape component is in\par
   memory can be changed much more rapidly.\par
\par
   Note: There is no file data mechanism per se; the system merely\par
   provides an orderly mechanism to save program images.  All actual\par
   user data must be maintained on paper-tape.\par
\par
The interaction with OS/8 design is that there is hardly ever any reason to save the contents of memory when using OS/8.  A contrived excuse is that because of the lack of a proper alternative mechanism, creating OS/8 program images must be accomplished by this mechanism.  While the seeds of the related OS/8 problems started in the design of the DECtape Library System, the design was further weakened in the Disk Monitor System.\par
\par
b) The Disk Monitor System runs on a 4K PDP-8 system with limited\par
   ability to load extended memory (must be performed as a non\par
   starting image limited to a single field at a time).\line\line    Note: The Disk Monitor System became self-obsolescent as it\par
   required devices capable of being expressed as multiples of 129\par
   words/block when in fact, only three early devices were capable of\par
   this.  The DF32 and RF08 fixed-head disks can be addressed to the\par
   nearest word; as such, block size is arbitrary (up to a maximum of\par
   4096 words).  The 129th word of DECtape is a requirement of the\par
   tape format because all DECtape blocks are required to be whole\par
   multiples of 18-bit words; all other PDP-8 operating systems are\par
   organized around whole multiples of 128 words (including OS/8 and\par
   P?S/8).\par
\par
   Despite the fact that system programs are hardly ever saved, all\par
   reload operations of the Disk Monitor System save a portion of\par
   memory to designated scratch blocks before loading in the keyboard\par
   monitor ro allow further user input.\par
\par
   By nature, system reloading is a risky process because during some\par
   portion of the process, the contents of memory is being changed\par
   using a process that cannot verify its own validity (and also does\par
   not provided recourse if an error actually occurred).  A good\par
   design should make system reloading a read-only process as much as\par
   possible; unfortunately, every reload operation starts with saving\par
   a portion of memory in the scratch blocks thus lowering the overall\par
   reliability at all times.\par
\par
The forerunner to what was eventually named OS/8 was presented to DEC management as a hack demo program written over a weekend in the midst of an extended brainstorming session over the course of months performed by the same people who wrote the R-L Monitor System (see below.)\page Because all of the relevant PDP-8 programmers responsible for much of the early works knew each other outside of the bounds of employment at DEC, a free discussion allowed arguing the merits of various design principles, some of which were shot down after proper discussion of the merits.\line\line The demo program merely represented an overview demo of what the project was all about (essentially reminiscent of a user's experience when using the PDP-10 TOPS1 operating system).  It was not intended as a product per se; the programmers were led to believe they had free reign to perform appropriate design and implementation as required.\par
Instead of the rational expectations of the programmers (who represent the finest programming minds ever to program the PDP-8), DEC management issued various edicts illustrating their profound ignorance of the process of operating system deign in general.\par
\par
Despite the haphazard nature of the frenzied work performed over a very sleepless weekend, the managers insisted that the design be nearly frozen!\par
\par
Note: As such, much of the design is half-baked and includes contradictory and dormant features that make no sense considering what management did to the design process.  (Some of these features exist in P?S/8 fully implemented.)\line\line It is surmised that managemen presumed that changing the coding might change change the way the system was approached by end users despite this being completely inaccurate.\line\par
Additionally, some of the limitations from the Disk Monitor design were demanded to be added (which is admittedly hypocritical and also self-defeating: By adding internal features the design is being modified).\par
\par
As such, OS/8 became needlessly memory-segmented with a minimal scratch space (which only makes sense in the case of the DECtape Library system).  Since the segment size is 1024 12-bit words, all functions must be implemented within that limited code space size.  Many features cannot be implemented due to this needless limitation.\par
\par
